\section{Related Work}

\subsection{VLA Safety and Spatial Representations}

Vision-language-action models achieve high task success but remain collision-prone~\cite{li2026vlasafety}. Safety-oriented approaches either filter actions at inference via control barrier functions~\cite{hu2025vlsa, zhai2026cofreevla, raja2024ogmcbf} or embed constraints during training~\cite{zhang2025safevla}; none examines whether spatial representations contribute to safety.

A separate line of work enriches VLAs with 3D structure to boost task success: depth fusion~\cite{yuan2025depthvla, li2025qdepthvla, rao2026augvla3d, bhat20253dcavla, abouzeid2025geoaware, yang2024depthanything}, representation alignment~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, wu2025spatialvla}, explicit geometric encoding~\cite{li2025pointvla, sun2025geovla, wu2026stvla, chen2026gstvla, tu2026sgvla, xiao2026stemvla, li2026corridorvla}, and occupancy prediction~\cite{liu2025occvla, li2022bevformer, sima2023occnet, tian2023occ3d}. At least fifteen such methods have appeared in the past year, all reporting improved task success; none evaluates collision avoidance. We address this gap.

\subsection{Auxiliary Tasks and Multi-Task Gradient Dynamics}

Multi-task learning exploits shared representations across objectives~\cite{caruana1997multitask, ruder2017overview}, but auxiliary tasks can cause negative transfer through both optimization conflict and generalization gap~\cite{chen2018gradnorm, yu2020pcgrad, liu2021cagrad, navon2022nashmtl, jiang2023forkmerge}. In overparameterized networks, multi-task training induces implicit regularization distinct from single-task fine-tuning~\cite{lippl2024implicit}; removing a gradient source can starve certain feature directions~\cite{pezeshki2021gradient}. BatchNorm statistics encode distributional assumptions that degrade performance when violated~\cite{li2017adabn, schneider2020bn}, making BN a sensitive channel for auxiliary task effects. For VLA fine-tuning with LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora}, the interaction between auxiliary losses and low-rank adapter updates has not been studied; our factorial design isolates gradient flow from spatial content and identifies an ordinal interaction that challenges the assumption that auxiliary spatial supervision should improve safety.

