The disconnect between TSR and CAR has a structural explanation: 7 of 10 tasks already exhibit CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla (ceiling effect), while the 3 high-collision tasks involve tight spatial constraints where collisions arise from physical proximity, not from a lack of spatial awareness. Spatial supervision addresses a different bottleneck than the one that drives collision failures (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

The $2{\times}2$ factorial design prioritizes breadth (12 conditions, 5 factors) over per-cell statistical power. With $n{\leq}4$ seeds per cell, post-hoc power for pairwise tests is limited ($\sim$0.3--0.4)~\cite{cohen1988statistical}. However, the directional consistency across all twelve conditions---every configuration performs at or below Vanilla---yields $p < 0.001$ under a binomial null, providing strong global evidence that auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve collision avoidance (Appendix~\ref{app:stat_power}).

